<div class="source"><p>Each estimate is for one year, not a running total. All were checked against their sources on {{ ("tl-cost-ifs-2023" | figure).checked_date | timeTag | safe }}, except the 2026-27 estimate by the Institute for Fiscal Studies, checked on {{ ("tl-cost-ifs-2026" | figure).checked_date | timeTag | safe }}. The estimates differ because they compare with different rules, measure different years and come from different forecasts. None is wrong because another is different.</p></div>

<p>The Office for Budget Responsibility also says the cost by 2029-30 is around three times what was first assumed. It gives {{ m.f("tl-cost-vs-earnings-2029-30") }} against {{ m.f("tl-cost-initial-assumption") }}. That compares two differences from earnings uprating. It does not mean State Pension spending is three times what was expected.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("tl-cost-initial-assumption" | figure) }}

<h3 id="saving">The saving from a change: small at first, large over decades</h3>

{% call m.note("Past cost is not future saving", 4) %}
<p>The increases of past years are part of today's rates. Changing the rule does not reverse them. A change only affects increases from the date it starts, so the saving begins small and grows.</p>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.scrollTable() %}
<table>
  <caption>Estimates of the saving from changing the yearly increase, compared with keeping the triple lock</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Change</th><th scope="col" class="num">Estimated saving</th><th scope="col">When</th><th scope="col">Basis</th><th scope="col">Who made it</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    {%- for id, change, suffix in [
      ["saving-rule-2030-late-2030s","The rule announced for April 2030"," a year"],
      ["saving-rule-2030-2050","The rule announced for April 2030"," a year"],
      ["saving-smoothed-rf-2030","Smoothed earnings link from 2027-28"," a year"],
      ["saving-earnings-gad-2031","Earnings link from April 2026",", built up over five years"],
      ["saving-earnings-ifs-2050","Pension kept at its present share of earnings from 2025"," a year"],
      ["saving-earnings-2075","Earnings link after 2030-31"," a year"],
      ["saving-cpi-2075","Prices link after 2030-31"," a year"]] %}
    {%- set fg = id | figure %}
    <tr><th scope="row">{{ change }}</th><td class="num phrase">{{ id | figShown }}{{ suffix }}</td><td>{{ fg.period_label }}</td><td>{{ fg.gross_net }}{% if fg.price_basis and fg.price_basis != "cash" %}. {{ fg.price_basis | upperFirst }}{% endif %}</td><td>{{ m.cite(fg.source) }}</td></tr>
    {%- endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
{% endcall %}
<div class="source"><p>Estimates and projections, not forecasts of what will happen. None describes a cut to pensions already in payment. The sources do not say so in those words. This is our reading of what each one models. Checked against the sources on {{ ("saving-earnings-2075" | figure).checked_date | timeTag | safe }}, except the two rows for the rule announced for April 2030, checked on {{ ("saving-rule-2030-2050" | figure).checked_date | timeTag | safe }}. The government's two figures are measured against keeping the triple lock, and cover Great Britain.</p></div>

<p>The pattern is consistent across sources. In the next few years the saving is small compared with the bill. Over decades it becomes large. The Institute for Fiscal Studies puts the cost of keeping the triple lock in 2050 at between {{ m.f("ifs-tl-cost-2050-low") }} and {{ m.f("ifs-tl-cost-2050-high") }} a year, a wide range because the result depends on how volatile prices and earnings turn out to be.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("ifs-tl-cost-2050-high" | figure) }}

<p>Spending would still rise without the triple lock. The Institute for Fiscal Studies says: "{{ ("ifs-rise-without-tl" | figure).passage }}".</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("ifs-rise-without-tl" | figure) }}

<h4 id="saving-2030">The rule announced for April 2030</h4>

<p>The table gives the government's estimates in cash, the money of each year. In the prices of 2025 to 2026 they are {{ m.f("saving-rule-2030-2040-real") }} for {{ ("saving-rule-2030-2040-real" | figure).period_label }} and {{ m.f("saving-rule-2030-2050-real") }} for {{ ("saving-rule-2030-2050-real" | figure).period_label }}.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("saving-rule-2030-2050-real" | figure) }}

<p>{{ ("ifs-savings-small-early" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ifs-savings-small-early") }} To show the scale, the Institute looks back. If the announced rule had applied since 2011, spending in 2026-27 would be {{ m.f("rule-2030-counterfactual-ifs-2026") }} lower. That is a look back, not a forecast.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("rule-2030-counterfactual-ifs-2026" | figure) }}

{% call m.note("How to read the government's figures", 5) %}
<p>{{ ("dwp-2030-baseline" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("dwp-2030-baseline") }} {{ ("dwp-2030-caution" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("dwp-2030-caution") }}</p>
<p>The estimate for {{ ("saving-rule-2030-2050-real" | figure).period_label }} in the prices of 2025 to 2026 is inside the Institute for Fiscal Studies' range, above, for what the triple lock itself would cost in 2050. The Institute gives its range in what it calls today's terms, as at 2025, so the two are close in kind but not on the same basis.</p>
<p>{{ ("no-rule-2030-detail" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("no-rule-2030-detail") }} So the estimates can be read, but not reproduced.</p>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.unavailable("costings for a double lock of prices or earnings, and for means-testing", 4) %}
<p>{{ ("no-costed-double-lock" | claim).statement }} {{ ("no-costed-means-test" | claim).statement }} We have not made our own estimates. A calculation that multiplied one person's difference by the number of pensioners would be wrong, because people receive different amounts and because tax and benefits change the result.</p>
{% endcall %}

<p>To see the State Pension beside other spending and taxes, the {{ m.sisterLink("budget-simulator" | link) }} lets you change each one. It is a simplified teaching model, not an official costing of any pension reform, and its State Pension figure is a forecast for an earlier year than the figures on this page. To put a figure in billions into scale, there is the {{ m.sisterLink("big-numbers" | link) }}. An amount per person from that tool is simple division. It is not what anyone pays or receives.</p>

<h2 id="who">Who receives what: incomes, wealth and poverty</h2>

<p>An average pensioner does not exist. The evidence below is about the spread.</p>

<h3 id="who-income">Income: the State Pension matters most to those with the lowest incomes</h3>

{{ m.dataTable("income-fifths") }}

<p>The State Pension matters most to people with the lowest incomes, and it still matters to those with the highest. Among recent retirees, it makes up {{ m.f("sp-share-income-poorest") }} of income for the poorest fifth and {{ m.f("sp-share-income-richest") }} for the richest fifth.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("sp-share-income-poorest" | figure) }}

<p>On this page, "low income" and "high income" mean a position in these distributions, unless a source's own definition is given.</p>

<h3 id="who-poverty">Poverty and deprivation: fewer pensioners are in low income once housing costs are counted</h3>

{%- set g = ["low-income-ahc", "low-income-bhc", "material-deprivation"] | shared %}
{{ m.sharedNote(g) }}
<ul class="cards cards--three">
  <li>{{ m.stat("low-income-ahc" | figure, "After rent, mortgage interest and similar costs are taken off.", 4, g) }}</li>
  <li>{{ m.stat("low-income-bhc" | figure, "Before housing costs are taken off.", 4, g) }}</li>
  <li>{{ m.stat("material-deprivation" | figure, "A measure of going without, not of income.", 4, g) }}</li>
</ul>

<p>Fewer pensioners are in low income after housing costs than before, because most own their home outright. {{ m.c("hbai-owners") }} For a pensioner who rents, housing costs work the other way.</p>

<h3 id="who-wealth">Housing and wealth: wealth is spread more unevenly than income</h3>

<p>Of households headed by someone aged 65 or over, {{ m.f("tenure-own-outright") }} own outright, {{ m.f("tenure-mortgage") }} are buying with a mortgage, {{ m.f("tenure-social-rent") }} rent from a social landlord and {{ m.f("tenure-private-rent") }} rent privately.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("tenure-own-outright" | figure) }}

{{ m.dataTable("wealth-bands") }}

<p>Wealth is spread more unevenly than income. Among single households over State Pension age, a quarter have total wealth below {{ m.f("wealth-single-p25") }} and a quarter have more than {{ m.f("wealth-single-p75") }}.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("wealth-single-p25" | figure) }}

{% call m.note("Income, wealth and need are three different things", 4) %}
<p>A person can have a valuable home and a low income. A home cannot pay a bill unless it is sold or borrowed against. A person can have a high income and high costs, for example for care. People with high income and people with high wealth are not the same group, and neither measure shows what a person needs.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h3 id="who-men-women">Differences between men and women</h3>

<p>Among people aged 65 to 74, the median private pension wealth of men is {{ m.f("pension-wealth-men-65-74") }} and of women is {{ m.f("pension-wealth-women-65-74") }}, counting people with none. Women on the older State Pension system also receive less on average than men. <a href="/state-pension/#what-people-receive">What people actually receive</a>. A person with little private pension depends more on the State Pension, and so is more exposed to a change in it.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("pension-wealth-women-65-74" | figure) }}

<h3 id="who-disability">Disability and caring</h3>

<p>{{ m.f("disabled-share-spa") }} of adults of State Pension age report a disability. {{ ("disability-income-artefact" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("disability-income-artefact") }}</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("disabled-share-spa" | figure) }}

